text:
The rewrite builds RichView core and its servers:
- the Rich Markdown format and style cascade, the chart engine and the report renderer;
- the CLI with MCP and `learn`, the VS Code extension, the browser playground and preview, and PDF export;
- RichView Cowork, the self-hostable internal server (INT-83), and RichView Publish (INT-35), which share their server core. RichView News is just one instance of Publish, not a separate product (INT-74);
- richview.ai with its docs and the flagship.

Also in scope: scrollytelling, as one more element inside a broader report (FEA-RP-48); author-hosted assets and uploads (FEA-SF-50, TYP-41); and RichView-managed hosting for large data and media, which RichView runs as a service (this changes INT-27). The LinkedIn carousel output is dropped (FEA-RP-46). Live connectors stay a secondary path of Publish (INT-24, OPN-63). Dashboards and the voice demo stay on their own cards (INT-33, INT-34).

Charts render with WebGL by default; RichView goes all in on it. Only PDF export converts charts to vector output, and that export may take time; it does not have to be instant.

As proposed, with these changes. Agents may also, without asking:
- delete other branches, not only their own merged task branch;
- set up automations themselves (for example workflows and scheduled jobs), within the other limits of this card;
- talk to other agents and collaborate on tasks: create communication tools such as message boards, and ping other agents with suggestions, through Linear and local helpers such as Herdr.
Everything else in the "Needs Daniel's explicit authorization" column stays as proposed.

RichView aims to be the richest chart library: it supports at least every chart type the major charting packages define, namely ggplot2, Plotly, ECharts, Highcharts and Power BI. That is likely around 30 types rather than nine; the exact list is compiled from those libraries.

Beyond charts, RichView adds:
- a map element;
- a flow element: flowcharts modelled on Nova flow, but done more meaningfully and fully ported to Rust;
- much better tables, with the best styling, as both normal tables and pivot tables.

Option 1, less strict. Each style gets one review sheet generated from the candidate. Show what makes sense rather than every variation:

- the typography specimen, the every-block fixture, a representative majority of chart types (not necessarily all), the map, flow, a normal table and the pivot table, and the flagship, at phone and desktop widths and as PDF;
- the same content in the other four styles beside it, so structural differences show: outlines and shading, legend and title positions, Scientific's captions below charts in boxes;
- the style's declared structure (where legends and titles sit, outlines or none) verified against the geometry model, and STY-52's measured checks;
- entrance clips in that style;
- STY-51's three answers.

The agents choose the selection. Daniel reviews a sheet when a style is created and when its tokens or structure change. A review agent on the model he uses at the time judges the sheet first, including distinctiveness beyond the mechanical check, and its verdict sits on the sheet (DEL-68). A mechanical check flags two styles whose structural declarations are identical. Once a style is accepted, later changes reach Daniel one by one through the before-and-after report (DEL-69).

Launch is simple: it happens when Daniel says so, and everything goes live at once. That covers:
- richview.ai drops "Coming soon";
- the try path (the playground, INT-71's install prompt and script, builds from `prod`, the extension on the VS Code Marketplace and Open VSX, and the source build);
- the flagship and the docs;
- the public source (INT-38).

No date is set, and nothing else gates it. Agents keep everything ready so his one word makes it all live. The first posts still go out only after Daniel has reviewed them.
